    declaration: struct-decl #40 2142:46:1-3105:64:1 name="@2::Connection" doc="An HTTP/1.1 connection that the program drives itself, as `Net::HTTP.start` gives: one request at a time, and the\nbody read piece by piece. Open it with `start`, send with `send_request`, read with `read_body` and end it with\n`finish`. The fields are the connection's own: do not read them. It belongs to the task that opened it, as a socket\ndoes, and one call at a time uses it."
      field: field-decl #25 2205:48:3-2224:49:1 name="socket" doc="The socket, a plain one or a TLS one."
        type: type-ref #24 2213:48:11-2223:48:21 name="TCP.Socket"
      field: field-decl #27 2303:50:3-2318:51:1 name="scheme" doc="`http` or `https`, the host and the port, which the request head names."
        type: type-ref #26 2311:50:11-2317:50:17 name="String"
      field: field-decl #29 2320:51:3-2333:52:1 name="host"
        type: type-ref #28 2326:51:9-2332:51:15 name="String"
      field: field-decl #31 2335:52:3-2345:53:1 name="port"
        type: type-ref #30 2341:52:9-2344:52:12 name="Int"
      field: field-decl #33 2640:56:3-2672:57:1 name="options" doc="The options it was opened with: `timeout_ms` is the time of each `send_request` (the write and the head),\n`read_timeout_ms` the time of each `read_body`, and `max_body_bytes` the largest request body. A `proxy` has no\nuser info here, so printing a connection shows no password."
        type: type-ref #32 2649:56:12-2671:56:34 name="HttpWire.ClientOptions"
      field: field-decl #36 2868:59:3-2891:60:1 name="login" doc="The `Proxy-Authorization` value of the proxy's user info (\"\" with none), alone in a channel that holds one value:\na channel prints as `<Channel>`, where a field would print the login."
        type: type-ref #35 2875:59:10-2890:59:25 name="Channel"
          arg: type-ref #34 2883:59:18-2889:59:24 name="String"
      field: field-decl #39 3077:62:3-3101:63:1 name="cell" doc="The one `Exchange` of the connection, in a channel that holds a single value: a call takes it and puts it back,\nso a second call at the same time finds it empty and says so."
        type: type-ref #38 3083:62:9-3100:62:26 name="Channel"
          arg: type-ref #37 3091:62:17-3099:62:25 name="@2::Exchange"
    declaration: struct-decl #46 3262:67:1-3461:73:1 name="@2::ResponseHead" doc="The head of a response read on a `Connection`: the `status` and `headers` (names in lowercase). The body is still\non the connection, for `read_body`."
      field: field-decl #42 3385:69:3-3397:70:1 name="status" doc="The status, however high: 404 and 500 are normal responses. An interim `1xx` head is read past."
        type: type-ref #41 3393:69:11-3396:69:14 name="Int"
      field: field-decl #45 3426:71:3-3457:72:1 name="headers" doc="The response headers."
        type: type-ref #44 3435:71:12-3456:71:33 name="List"
          arg: type-ref #43 3440:71:17-3455:71:32 name="HttpWire.Header"
    declaration: struct-decl #59 4356:82:1-4471:88:1 name="@2::Stream" doc="A response whose body arrives on a channel, from `open`: the `status` and `headers` of the head, the `body` channel\nand the `done` task that feeds it. The task reads the connection and sends each piece (up to 64 KiB, never empty),\nand it closes the channel on every path, so the reader runs the usual receive loop until the closed error and then\nawaits `done`: `ok` means the body ended cleanly, an `error` says why it did not (a cut body, a timeout, a body over\n256 MiB). To stop early the reader closes the channel: the task's next `send` fails, it closes the connection and\n`done` is an `error`. That works only while data flows: on a quiet stream the task waits in its read until\n`read_timeout_ms` (for ever when it is 0), so the prompt stop is `Task.cancel(stream.done)`, which closes the\nconnection at once. The connection belongs to the task and is never reused."
      field: field-decl #48 4372:83:3-4384:84:1 name="status"
        type: type-ref #47 4380:83:11-4383:83:14 name="Int"
      field: field-decl #51 4386:84:3-4417:85:1 name="headers"
        type: type-ref #50 4395:84:12-4416:84:33 name="List"
          arg: type-ref #49 4400:84:17-4415:84:32 name="HttpWire.Header"
      field: field-decl #54 4419:85:3-4440:86:1 name="body"
        type: type-ref #53 4425:85:9-4439:85:23 name="Channel"
          arg: type-ref #52 4433:85:17-4438:85:22 name="Bytes"
      field: field-decl #58 4442:86:3-4467:87:1 name="done"
        type: type-ref #57 4448:86:9-4466:86:27 name="Task"
          arg: type-ref #56 4453:86:14-4465:86:26 name="Result"
            arg: type-ref #55 4460:86:21-4464:86:25 name="Void"
    declaration: struct-decl #72 4919:93:1-5944:108:1 name="@2::StreamResponse" doc="A response whose body is sent as it is produced: the `status` and `headers` of the head, and a `body` channel of\npieces. The handler returns it at once and a task it spawned sends the pieces and closes the channel at the end. The\nserver sends each piece as soon as it arrives and ends the body when the channel closes. When the client leaves, the\nserver closes the channel, so the producer's next `send` returns an error and it stops."
      field: field-decl #61 5036:95:3-5048:96:1 name="status" doc="The status, 200 to 599, and not 204 or 304 unless the body is already closed and empty."
        type: type-ref #60 5044:95:11-5047:95:14 name="Int"
      field: field-decl #64 5169:97:3-5200:98:1 name="headers" doc="The response headers. The server adds `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` or `Content-Length`: the handler sets neither."
        type: type-ref #63 5178:97:12-5199:97:33 name="List"
          arg: type-ref #62 5183:97:17-5198:97:32 name="HttpWire.Header"
      field: field-decl #67 5284:99:3-5305:100:1 name="body" doc="The pieces of the body, as `Bytes` of any content (an empty one is skipped)."
        type: type-ref #66 5290:99:9-5304:99:23 name="Channel"
          arg: type-ref #65 5298:99:17-5303:99:22 name="Bytes"
      field: field-decl #71 5911:106:3-5940:107:1 name="length" doc="The size of the whole body in bytes, when the handler knows it (a file). The head then says `Content-Length` and\nthe pieces go out as they are, with no chunk framing, so a client can show progress and a range answer can\nstream. The producer must send exactly that many bytes and then close the channel: a body that ends short, or\na piece that would pass the length, cuts the connection (the client sees an incomplete body), and a body that is\nclosed when the handler returns and has another size is a 500. Not for a 204 or a 304. `none` is the default: the\nbody is chunked."
        type: type-ref #69 5919:106:11-5932:106:24 name="Optional"
          arg: type-ref #68 5928:106:20-5931:106:23 name="Int"
        default: call #70 5935:106:27-5939:106:31 name="none" piped=false
    declaration: type-alias-decl #77 6119:111:1-6182:112:1 name="@2::Handler" doc="A route that takes the request whole: the request in, the whole response out. `app` makes an `App` from it. A\nmiddleware is a function from a `Handler` to a `Handler`."
      target: type-ref #76 6134:111:16-6181:111:63 name="Fn"
        arg: type-ref #73 6137:111:19-6153:111:35 name="HttpWire.Request"
        arg: type-ref #75 6155:111:37-6180:111:62 name="Result"
          arg: type-ref #74 6162:111:44-6179:111:61 name="HttpWire.Response"
    declaration: struct-decl #86 6764:118:1-6839:122:1 name="@2::RequestBody" doc="The body of a request as it arrives, on a channel, for an `App`: the `pieces` channel (`Bytes` of any content, up\nto 64 KiB each, never empty, a chunked body already decoded) and the `done` task that feeds it. The task closes the\nchannel on every path, so the app runs the usual receive loop until the closed error and then awaits `done`: `ok`\nmeans the body arrived whole, an `error` says why it did not (the client left, a bad chunk, a timeout, a body over\n`max_body_bytes`). An app that answers before the whole body arrived ends the connection after its answer."
      field: field-decl #81 6785:119:3-6808:120:1 name="pieces"
        type: type-ref #80 6793:119:11-6807:119:25 name="Channel"
          arg: type-ref #79 6801:119:19-6806:119:24 name="Bytes"
      field: field-decl #85 6810:120:3-6835:121:1 name="done"
        type: type-ref #84 6816:120:9-6834:120:27 name="Task"
          arg: type-ref #83 6821:120:14-6833:120:26 name="Result"
            arg: type-ref #82 6828:120:21-6832:120:25 name="Void"
    declaration: enum-decl #100 7638:130:1-7753:138:1 name="@2::Message" type-parameters=[] doc="A message of a WebSocket, in either direction. `Text` and `Binary` are whole messages: the server joins the\nfragments of a message it receives, and a text message is checked as UTF-8. `Ping` and `Pong` carry at most 125\nbytes. The server answers every ping it receives with a pong, and does not hand it to the handler. `Close` is the\nend: the code (1000 to 1003 and 1007 to 1014 are the registered codes, 3000 to 4999 are for programs) and a reason\nof at most 123 bytes. It is always the last message the handler receives, and when the connection ended with no\nclose frame (the peer left, a timeout) its code is 1006. `Prepared` is a text or binary message already framed by\n`prepare`, so a broadcast does not frame its message for every socket; the server never receives one."
      variant: enum-variant #88 7653:131:3-7666:132:1 name="Text"
        payload: type-ref #87 7658:131:8-7664:131:14 name="String"
      variant: enum-variant #90 7668:132:3-7682:133:1 name="Binary"
        payload: type-ref #89 7675:132:10-7680:132:15 name="Bytes"
      variant: enum-variant #92 7684:133:3-7696:134:1 name="Ping"
        payload: type-ref #91 7689:133:8-7694:133:13 name="Bytes"
      variant: enum-variant #94 7698:134:3-7710:135:1 name="Pong"
        payload: type-ref #93 7703:134:8-7708:134:13 name="Bytes"
      variant: enum-variant #97 7712:135:3-7731:136:1 name="Close"
        payload: type-ref #95 7718:135:9-7721:135:12 name="Int"
        payload: type-ref #96 7723:135:14-7729:135:20 name="String"
      variant: enum-variant #99 7733:136:3-7749:137:1 name="Prepared"
        payload: type-ref #98 7742:136:12-7747:136:17 name="Bytes"
    declaration: struct-decl #109 8445:145:1-8543:150:1 name="@2::WebSocket" doc="An upgraded connection, for the session function of `accept_websocket`: messages from the peer arrive on `incoming`\nand the messages to send go on `outgoing`. `protocol` is the subprotocol that was agreed, or \"\". Both channels hold\n8 messages, so a handler that does not read makes the server stop reading the socket, and a handler that sends\nfaster than the peer reads waits. A session must keep taking messages from `incoming` (or end): while it does not,\nthe server does not read the socket, so the pings and the close of the peer go unanswered. `incoming` closes after\nthe `Close` message, and `outgoing` closes when the connection is over, so a send then is an error."
      field: field-decl #103 8464:146:3-8491:147:1 name="incoming"
        type: type-ref #102 8474:146:13-8490:146:29 name="Channel"
          arg: type-ref #101 8482:146:21-8489:146:28 name="@2::Message"
      field: field-decl #106 8493:147:3-8520:148:1 name="outgoing"
        type: type-ref #105 8503:147:13-8519:147:29 name="Channel"
          arg: type-ref #104 8511:147:21-8518:147:28 name="@2::Message"
      field: field-decl #108 8522:148:3-8539:149:1 name="protocol"
        type: type-ref #107 8532:148:13-8538:148:19 name="String"
    declaration: type-alias-decl #114 8819:154:1-8871:155:1 name="@2::WebSocketSession" doc="The function that runs a WebSocket after its handshake: it gets the `WebSocket` and returns when it is done. When it\nreturns, the server sends a close frame (code 1000, or 1011 for an `error`) if the session did not, waits for the\npeer's, and closes the connection."
      target: type-ref #113 8843:154:25-8870:154:52 name="Fn"
        arg: type-ref #110 8846:154:28-8855:154:37 name="@2::WebSocket"
        arg: type-ref #112 8857:154:39-8869:154:51 name="Result"
          arg: type-ref #111 8864:154:46-8868:154:50 name="Void"
    declaration: struct-decl #135 9794:164:1-9975:172:1 name="@2::WebSocketOptions" doc="The limits of a WebSocket. `max_message_bytes` is the largest message, joined from its fragments (1 to 256 MiB): a\nlarger one closes the connection with code 1009. `idle_ms` is the longest the server waits for the peer to send\nanything, 0 for no limit but a year (a read that times out closes the socket); a timeout drops the connection, and\nthe handler receives `Close` with code 1006. `ping_ms` makes the server send a ping after that many milliseconds\nwithout anything to write, 0 for none: with a `ping_ms` below `idle_ms` the pongs keep a live peer from being\ndropped. `close_ms` is how long the server waits for the peer's answer to its close frame. `protocols` are the\nsubprotocols the server speaks, in order of preference. `outgoing_queue` is how many messages `outgoing` holds (1 to\n1048576) before a send waits: the writer takes everything queued at that moment and writes it in one go."
      field: field-decl #118 9820:165:3-9853:166:1 name="max_message_bytes"
        type: type-ref #116 9839:165:22-9842:165:25 name="Int"
        default: literal #117 9845:165:28-9852:165:35 value=int:1048576
      field: field-decl #121 9855:166:3-9872:167:1 name="idle_ms"
        type: type-ref #119 9864:166:12-9867:166:15 name="Int"
        default: literal #120 9870:166:18-9871:166:19 value=int:0
      field: field-decl #124 9874:167:3-9891:168:1 name="ping_ms"
        type: type-ref #122 9883:167:12-9886:167:15 name="Int"
        default: literal #123 9889:167:18-9890:167:19 value=int:0
      field: field-decl #127 9893:168:3-9914:169:1 name="close_ms"
        type: type-ref #125 9903:168:13-9906:168:16 name="Int"
        default: literal #126 9909:168:19-9913:168:23 value=int:5000
      field: field-decl #131 9916:169:3-9945:170:1 name="protocols"
        type: type-ref #129 9927:169:14-9939:169:26 name="List"
          arg: type-ref #128 9932:169:19-9938:169:25 name="String"
        default: list-lit #130 9942:169:29-9944:169:31
      field: field-decl #134 9947:170:3-9971:171:1 name="outgoing_queue"
        type: type-ref #132 9963:170:19-9966:170:22 name="Int"
        default: literal #133 9969:170:25-9970:170:26 value=int:8
    declaration: enum-decl #143 10203:175:1-10308:179:1 name="@2::Upgrade" type-parameters=[] doc="What `accept_websocket` decides for a request, for an `Answer.Upgrade`: `Reply` answers it as a whole response (and\nthe connection serves the next request), `Accept` upgrades it. Make an `Accept` with `accept_websocket`."
      variant: enum-variant #137 10218:176:3-10243:177:1 name="Reply"
        payload: type-ref #136 10224:176:9-10241:176:26 name="HttpWire.Response"
